  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Mild conditions continue through Thursday.\n\n- Patchy valley fog in the mornings, especially in narrow valleys.\n\n- Significant change coming Friday into the weekend.\n\nA weak, but broad ridge over the region combined with less solar\nheating this time of year is causing very stable conditions across\nthe region. While this is causing overall mild weather and sunny\nafternoons, it's also allowing valley cold pools to develop with\nintermittent fog in the mornings. This is expected to continue\nthrough Thursday. \n\nThursday night into the weekend a trough and cold front move into\nthe region. Wind gusts around 20 mph are expected in Idaho and\n20-30 mph across most of western Montana on Friday. Most of the\nmoisture with this system will move up from the south and affect\nsouthwest Montana, but some light showers are also expected across\nnorthwest Montana. Most of the precipitation will be in the warm\nsector, and while snow levels will fall to around 5,000 ft Friday\nand lower over the weekend, most of the moisture will come ahead\nof that and fall as rain. Forecast snow amounts across the region\nare generally less than an inch, and mostly above 5,000 ft. The\nexception of course is the higher elevations of Flathead National\nForest and Glacier National Park, which will likely pick up\nseveral inches of snow this weekend. Recent conditions have been\nvery warm and mild, so while this change represents a big change\nin the weather, it's not expected to be much of an impact.",
